checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-editor.md:48:3. **Where did pay stand as the next Fed meeting approached?** The [September release](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:303>) says “Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent.” This changes **answer 2** and should replace part of the date list in item 2, rather than lengthen it.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:9:7. The next five questions: Partly. "Who the 4.2 percent left out" gets only the Black unemployment rate: "the rate for Black workers rose a full point in one month, to 7 percent."
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:16:- (new) The chart lists July -10,000 and August +133,000. The text only gives August's first figure, 162,000, and says "60,000 lower". The revised monthly figures are never stated, so I could not reconcile them.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:17:- (repeat, r0) "so most of the visible tenth is rounding". The unrounded 4.141% to 4.175% is a move of 0.034 points. I had to work out what that does to the 0.1 headline change.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:18:- (repeat, r0) Three averages: "That 45,000 is the prior 12 months; the Associated Press put average gains at 68,000 jobs a month so far this year, and 2025 at 9,700 jobs a month." Nothing says how they relate.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:21:- (repeat, r0) ADP's "90,000 jobs" gives no reason for the gap with 46,000, and it sits beside Reuters' 90,000 forecast.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:22:- (repeat, r0) The Black rate is used as the one group left out, with no reason for the choice.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:30:- (new) "Unrounded, a St. Louis Fed note dated Oct. 2, 2026, says..." is introduced by a dateline and a bare "Unrounded", which reads like a note to self.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r3-stranger.md:11:7. The next five questions: **Partly.** "who the 4.2 percent left out" gets only "the rate for Black workers rose a full point in one month, to 7 percent." No reason is given for choosing that group.
