checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:1:## First read
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:3:**Story in one sentence:** September payrolls rose by an estimated 29,000, below forecasts, but BLS described payrolls and unemployment as little changed and revised July and August down by 60,000.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:7:**First lost thread:** “Average hourly earnings increased by 3 percent over the past 12 months. On September 16 the Federal Reserve's committee decided to…”
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:20:4. **Two surveys: yes.** The first and last sentences of the marked paragraph carry the mechanism.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:21:5. **What changed: yes, with effort.** The marked passages contain the comparisons, but the forecast named in the headline arrives only after the chart.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:26:The headline’s “surveys” can mean either forecasts or the two BLS surveys explained later. It uses “not statistically significant” before giving a cold reader its meaning. The dek’s “that gain” depends on the long headline. The first body sentence says that BLS published a report, not what it found. No person is named before introduction. The top needs the result first and the forecast comparison close to it.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:32:2. **Major · new · MOVE.** “On Friday, October 2, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern, the Bureau of Labor Statistics published the Employment Situation for September.” Put the reported result before release logistics so the first sentence says what happened.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:38:5. **Minor · new · CUT.** ““The household survey measures labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry.”” The surrounding two sentences already explain which survey supplies each headline figure.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:44:8. **Minor · residual · CUT.** ““A Labor Department measure of gross hiring – before subtracting those who quit or lose their jobs – has been stuck in a rut for more than two years.” That hiring count, before quits and job losses are subtracted, is not the payroll change of 29,000.” This outlet detour uses more words than the distinction the reader needs; use the pinned JOLTS record for the unresolved September count. [Patch ledger](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md:716>).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:46:9. **Major · new · MOVE.** “A Reuters survey of economists had nonfarm payrolls likely up by 90,000 last month.” The headline invokes forecasts long before the page identifies one.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:48:10. **Minor · new · CUT.** “Estimates in that survey ran from 35,000 to 180,000. September's gain of 29,000 is below the low of 35,000.” The 90,000 forecast already supplies the comparison; the range belongs in proof.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r1-redteam.md:3:2. **Minor · new: the page credits an outlet for averages available in its pinned primary data.** Page bytes: “the Associated Press put average gains at 68,000 jobs a month so far this year, and 2025 at 9,700 jobs a month.” The pinned [PAYEMS series](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/PAYEMS-2026-10-03.csv:1033) gives `2024-12-01,158316`, `2025-12-01,158432`, and `2026-09-01,159044`. Those levels yield about 9,700 per month in 2025 and exactly 68,000 per month through September 2026. The [AP pin](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/coverage/ap-september-jobs.txt) states the same averages; it is not a separate data lineage for them.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:3:The [built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html) reports the headline numbers and revisions correctly, but its default **The story** mode hides the detailed sourced blocks. That leaves readers without the sampling limit on the 29,000 estimate, the household survey’s countervailing gains, and the sharp rise in Black unemployment. It also calls a change in **net payroll jobs** a slowdown in **hiring**, which is a different measure. The facts view contains a separate 1,000-fold unit error.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:19:2. **Major, grade B: the rounded unemployment rise has countervailing evidence.** Page bytes: “unemployment ticks up.” In the [BLS household summary](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:328), the exact rows show labor force change “485,” employed change “406,” and unemployed change “78,” all in thousands. [St. Louis Fed calculations](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september) put the unrounded rate at “4.1413%” in August and “4.1754%” in September. The correct account is a small increase in unemployment **while employment and participation also rose**. This changes the easiest wrong reading of the headline. Those household figures appear in Just the facts, but the default story hides them.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:21:3. **Major, grade B: the national rate conceals a substantial group difference.** Page bytes: “unemployment rate was 4.2 percent.” The [BLS summary row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:348) reads “Black or African American | 7.6 | 6.3 | 6.0 | 7.0 | 1.0.” The correct account is that Black unemployment rose **one percentage point from August to September**, even as the national rounded rate moved one tenth. The story view should carry that distributional change. The same row shows 7.0 percent remained below September 2025’s 7.6 percent, so this is a monthly finding, not a claim of yearlong deterioration.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:35:**Around:** The [BLS household table](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:328), [ADP report](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September), and [St. Louis Fed flow analysis](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september) test different parts of the headline. They should be reconciled by measure and population, not counted as votes for one interpretation.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:45:| **Minor, grade C:** diffusion-index definition | “the share of 250 private industries adding jobs” | The [BLS footnote](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:440) includes “plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment.” The index is **49.0 under that formula**, not simply the share adding jobs. This is in Just the facts and does not change a seven-answer conclusion. |
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:46:| **Verified correction already on the page** | “CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. That figure is mislabeled.” | The [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:305) says “remained at 34.4 hours.” No additional finding. |
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:48:**Passage grades, grouped by the answer they can change:** A: BLS headline and July/August revisions, carried in the story. B: survey uncertainty, household balance, Black unemployment, private/government split, historical baseline, gross-hiring distinction, and policy context; these are hidden or absent as detailed above. C: U-6, prime-age participation, longer-term unemployment, wage arithmetic, forecasts, and detailed industry rows; where supplied, Just the facts or Show the work is sufficient. D: release logistics and repetitive coverage that change none of the seven answers. Table A-9’s multiple-jobholder passage would ordinarily be C, but its **incorrect unit** makes the accuracy error Major.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:52:The page lists 21 records, but record count is not independent confirmation count. The BLS release and its tables are the primary payroll and household records; FRED and ALFRED reproduce BLS series. The page correctly labels PBS as “the same Associated Press dispatch, carried by PBS,” so those two pages are one reporting lineage. CNBC’s preview and follow-up reuse the Dow Jones forecast. The 84,000 and 90,000 forecasts are survey results reported by outlets, not independent observations of employment. [Manifest](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/manifests/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.json), [built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:58:**Established:** On October 2, BLS estimated **29,000 more nonfarm payroll jobs** in September, revised July and August down by a combined **60,000**, and reported **4.2 percent** rounded unemployment. Private payrolls rose **46,000** while government payrolls fell **17,000**. The household survey showed more people employed and participating, alongside more unemployed people; Black unemployment rose from **6.0 to 7.0 percent**. [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:225).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:60:**Strongly supported interpretation:** The payroll side weakened relative to the revised August result and forecasts, but the month does not establish a broad employment contraction or a measured drop in gross hiring. The unrounded unemployment change was small, and a separate private payroll measure was stronger. [BLS technical note](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:706), [St. Louis Fed](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september), [ADP](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September).
