codex
1. **Minor · new · FactSet forecast lacks its primary record.** Page bytes: “CBS reported a FactSet forecast of 90,000.” ([Built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html)) The [pinned CBS text](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/coverage/cbs-september-jobs.txt) says “according to financial data firm FactSet.” [FactSet’s October 1 record](https://insight.factset.com/total-nonfarm-payrolls-for-september-2026-are-projected-to-rise-by-90000) says “The median estimate for total nonfarm payroll employment for the month of September 2026 is 90,000” and that it is “based on 27 estimates collected by FactSet.” The page attributes CBS accurately, but the available primary forecast is absent from its [manifest](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/manifests/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.json).

2. **Minor · new · Wage-history fact rests on pinned primary data.** Page bytes: “The Associated Press called that yearly wage gain ‘the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021’.” ([Built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html)) The wording matches the [AP pin](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/coverage/ap-september-jobs.txt). The pinned [BLS wage series](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/CES0500000003-2026-10-03.csv) gives `2025-09-01,36.70` and `2026-09-01,37.81`; their change is 3.0245%. All 63 intervening annual comparisons in that series, from June 2021 through August 2026, are higher. AP supplies the quoted wording; the historical comparison is also verifiable from the pinned primary data.

VERDICT: SHIP
